\name{measurementPublication}
\alias{publishMeasurementRun}
\alias{currentMeasurementBundle}
\title{Publish and Resolve Approved Synthetic Measurement Results}
\usage{
publishMeasurementRun(runDir, publicationDir, approvalId,
                      outputScope = "restricted", institutionalPolicy = NULL)
currentMeasurementBundle(publicationDir)
}
\arguments{
\item{runDir}{Trusted local successful run generated by the current 15-output measurement executor.}
\item{publicationDir}{Dedicated local publication store, outside run storage. A new store must be empty. Restricted and distributable scopes require separate stores.}
\item{approvalId}{Explicit caller approval reference: 1--128 letters, digits, dots, underscores or hyphens, starting with a letter or digit. Do not put secrets or personal information in this reference.}
\item{outputScope}{\code{restricted} retains all validated fixture aggregates. \code{distributable} applies the conservative synthetic-aggregates-v1 projection.}
\item{institutionalPolicy}{Optional trusted function accepting a list with \code{manifest} and \code{content_sha256}. Only an exact \code{TRUE} permits publication; errors deny it. The callback cannot expand the built-in projection. It is not persisted or evaluated by viewing.}
}
\description{Validate the full requested manifest, exact typed values, cross-document semantics, checksums and ordinary-file inventory before committing a new local release. Only successfully executed marked synthetic PostgreSQL runs are accepted. Explicit caller approval is required for either output scope.}
\details{Requires Node.js 22.12 or newer, but no npm installation: the R package ships a precompiled standalone validator. Contract 1.0 remains frozen; completed executor bundles use contract 1.1. Metric definitions and numerical semantics are unchanged. A successful execution remains partial until approved publication. Distributable output retains the all-source person count, a fixed synthetic source passport and output-state metadata. All other partitions are explicitly suppressed with NULL rows, terminology is empty, source labels are replaced and no run ledger or configuration is copied. This deliberately narrow fixture policy is not an anonymization method for institutional data. A hook may apply additional institutional approval requirements but does not enable real-data execution or unrestricted export.

A store lock admits one publisher. A same-filesystem rename finalizes an immutable release directory, then another rename replaces current.json without deleting its predecessor first. Failed validation or publication cannot switch the pointer. A crash between these renames may leave an unreferenced complete release. Existing releases are never overwritten by this API. Stale locks require manual inspection; retry/resume, garbage collection, hostile-writer protection, network-filesystem and power-loss durability are not provided. Immutability is an API convention, not a guarantee against manual edits; current resolution validates bytes and hashes again. Durable source identity remains unknown and input reuse stays disabled. View a resolved release with \code{viewResults}; viewing a release itself requires no Node.js.}
\value{Publication invisibly returns the finalized release path. Resolution returns the fully validated current complete release path. Errors leave the previous current pointer unchanged; an unreferenced release may remain if the final pointer switch fails.}
\seealso{\code{planMeasurementRun}, \code{executeMeasurementRun}, \code{viewResults}}
